Hello dear annotator! 

I will give you a set of images representing activities from a timetable.

I will also give you a schema that your final answer should follow and a set of guidelines.

Consider that schema as a form that needs to be filled out using the information in the timetable.

Schema for the final answer, written as TypeScript code. The answer should be a JSON object that follows the shape of the Response interface:

<Schema>
interface Response {
	parsed_activities: Activity[];
}

// An entry in the timetable representing a course, lab etc
interface Activity {
   id: number;
	name: string;
	type?: "Curs" | "Lab" | "Seminar" | "Conferinta";
	authors: string[];
	location?: Location;
	periodicity?: "even" | "odd" | "range";
	subgroup?: number;
	observations?: string;
}

type Location = "ONLINE" | Room;

interface Room {
	id: number;
	type: "Amf" | "Lab" | "Sala";
}
</Schema>



Your answers should include snippets from the provided images. Do not ever add / remove diacritics.

Please follow the guidelines below. They contain notes on how to properly fill out each field for each activity of the final answer. Thank you very much for your work!!



ID FIELD
---

Each given activity has an ID that was specified before the photo was provided in a message like this: "Image with ID={id}".

The corresponding entry in the final answer for that activity should include that value in the `id` field.

Moreover, each image was given in the ascending order of their id. Similarly, the Activity entry corresponding to each image MUST appear in the order of ids in the final answer.



NAME FIELD
---

The name of a specific activity usually appears in the center of the image (though this is not guaranteed as the visual representation of other activities might be different).

The name could contain additional text serving various purposes (like specifying the type of the activity). Please do not include such additional info in the `name` field.
For example: 
	"MachineTranslation (curs)" -> the `name` should only be "MachineTranslation"
	"SS - Algebra comutativa" -> the `name` should only be "Algebra comutativa"

Be aware that, due to image quality, you may find yourself in a situation where you interpret the letter 'I' (upper case 'I') as an 'l' (lowercase 'l'). In this case, look for contextual clues to determine whether or not the uppercase 'I' was a reading mistake. If it was a reading mistake, replace that lowercase 'l' with an uppercase 'I' (DO NOT replace it with a lowercase 'i', always replace it with an uppercase 'I'). Note that in this specific edge case (interpreting an uppercase 'I' as a lowercase 'l' due to image quality) and only here, you MUST write what makes sense, not what is in the image. 

For example, if you see 'TopSplngSoft', ask yourself "Does that l, makes sense? Let's see what happens when I change it with an 'I': TopSpIngSoft". Because that "IngSoft" part might stand for "Inginerie Software", it makes more sense that it was a reading error and you should absolutely fill the `name` field with "TopSpIngSoft" instead of "TopSplngSoft"



(ACTIVITY) TYPES FIELD
---
 
Each activity has an OPTIONAL type. The types are defined as follows:
- `Conferinta`: SEMIFORMAL didactic activity, where at least one professor teaches students about a specific subject. It is semiformal because participation didn't require a registration at the university's office. Furthermore, these are usually open to the public and participation doesn't provide any credits. All activities in "Conferinte si Seminarii" are Conferinte by this definition (even though they might have "Seminar" in name)
- `Curs`, `Lab`, `Seminar`: a formal didactic activity for which the student solicited the right of participation from the university. Most of the activities are of this type.

Usually the type can be deduced from the name. For example, an activity like "ReteleNeuronale1 (curs)" will be of type `Curs`.

If the type of an activity is not obvious or cannot be determined with high confidence, set it to undefined / do not specify it. NEVER specify it if you are unsure. DO NOT SET THE TYPE IF UNSURE!



AUTHORS FIELD
---

Most activities are taught by at least one teacher. If an activity is taught by multiple teachers, the visual representation for that activity WILL have multiple colors.

Usually, the name of the teacher is in the upper left corner of the visual representation. However, please note that this is not a guarantee.

Author names follow this convention "<last name> <first letter (or, rarely, the first few letters) of the first name>". For example: "Nisioi S". 
If the activity is taught by multiple teachers, the names are separated by a forward slash. For example: "Leustean L / Irofti P / Patrascu A", "Marin Li / Tigoiu V"



LOCATION FIELD
--- 

The location is usually found in the bottom right corner of the visual representation of the activity. However, this is not guaranteed.

The location is, most of the time, written in a format like this: "S-415", "Amf.503", "L-202", "S.109", "L.218". As you can see, each of them has a room id, that uniquely identifies the room (the "415" in "S-415") and a small text providing info on the type of room (for example, the "L" in "L.218" suggests this room is of type "Lab"). The most common such snippets are:
"S" -> "Sala",
"L" -> "Lab",
"Amf" -> "Amfiteatru"
If you find a new location type that doesn't appear in the answer's schema, set it to undefined and note your discovery in the `observations` field.



PERIODICITY FIELD
---

Some context on the given timetable: some activities run on a biweekly schedule. In this case, the activity will happen either on an even or an odd week (which week is even or odd is decided by the university and later communicated to the students).

Now the actual guidelines:

If the visual representation of the activity has a specifier like "SP" (Saptamana Para -> `even`) or "SI" (Saptamana Impara -> `odd`) the work is done. Other times, the visual representation is even more explicit and contains stuff like "impar" or "saptamana para". Whatever the case, if the periodicity is specified via text, you can move on to filling other fields.

If the activity specifies a range of weeks (like "[sapt 9-15]"), then the activity IS NOT PERIODIC and DOES NOT run on a biweekly schedule. In this case, do NOT leave the field as undefined. Instead, you MUST set it to "range". This is the only time you set this field to "range". In no other case, should this value be used. 



SUBGROUP FIELD
---

Some context on FMI before going into the notes. Students are divided in groups and groups are identified via a 3-digit number (for example: "Grupa 461", "Grupa 152" etc). Because a group has a lot of students, these groups are divided in subgroups (usually 2 subgroups for Bachelor groups and 4 subgroups for the Master - though this is not guaranteed and can change). Most of the activities are for the entire group, however, a large portion of activities are for a specific subgroup only. If that's the case, populate the `subgroup` field with the number of that specific subgroup (which is always strictly greater than 0).

Now the actual guidelines:

Whenever you see "SG", know it means "subgroup". Similarly, "Gr_3" means "subgroup no. 3" (don't be fooled by that "Gr" - it is not a group, it is a subgroup; a group has a 3 digit identifier).



OBSERVATIONS FIELD
---

We are still developing the rules for how to properly parse the timetable. 
If you encounter any inconsistencies between the rules specified here (or the response specs) and the actual data (for example, if you find an activity that is not taught by a professor, even though we specified that every activity should be taught by at least one professor OR you encounter a new type of location that wasn't specified in the schema), record them in the optional `observations` field.
Moreover, in order to optimize the rules, if you were ever faced with a hard decision (like choosing between the `Conferinta` type and the `Seminar` type) that required you to think more than usual, complain about it in the `observations` field.
If neither of these happened, leave the `observations` field blank.



